[{:db/id "q-334148/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8d3fcb83-dcff-5edf-9e76-a684d5f68dde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334148/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2894756b-60dd-5e1f-b16c-6d93e3c681cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334148/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "fcafc1f0-a79a-5c22-aa55-2bfec22bf162" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334148/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "69b0b65d-894c-5799-8351-3bcd6383f33b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334148/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "547244de-598b-55fd-abcd-85754a87f3a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334148/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1759dba0-2b4d-56e3-88f7-63158716ed62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334148/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-334148/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-334148/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-334148/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-334148/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-334148/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334148" :question/id #uuid "322a6327-4647-5c0f-a844-3881dd52b8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-334148" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{2}$\n- $x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′}-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{4}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334148/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334148"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6b0e341a-5a36-57d8-a5aa-7ccfef0d4d74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9f4f39a8-b1fb-5ee1-a94f-9563971ab8a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1a051469-73a6-5a14-a502-84d108b9a1be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "06615766-a1ab-5c84-a233-59e916a01d2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "03fec8ac-3611-57c7-a479-fb76f9e00cf1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2963609a-310d-519d-b5b0-ca99ea28b833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334000/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-334000/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-334000/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-334000/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-334000/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-334000/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334000" :question/id #uuid "97007657-3a43-530e-8e1c-1dfd8246c0ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-334000" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$\n- $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334000/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334000"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b795ce81-4847-5bd6-a65b-6641046ef739" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2b1a441b-1287-57b3-a284-7e22fa166395" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c35c24d0-d7fe-5d15-825c-d76679c88b47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "4ac272a8-234d-5440-8800-c6f99546ad99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "960857cf-e714-5b47-b9bf-ee4c37ba8eb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5367a1bf-7fac-5198-8293-389cb8e50f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334133/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-334133/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-334133/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-334133/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-334133/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-334133/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334133" :question/id #uuid "17c86934-9bcf-5735-a8b5-c941974ac22b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334133" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$\n- ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334133/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334133"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-321228"] :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}-y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{2}+2xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "931f9b6b-fb51-5532-afae-cb69fe0718dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "cf27b51b-1135-5277-a0d1-0bce6ca54618" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4e502a6a-0a25-52b4-954b-5e4f41c2b638" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "10b09b93-ae6c-50eb-a181-680385b00433" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "372a4852-a0f8-5082-a868-97c444a1ce70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bee02561-94bc-51a3-92e1-ef402611f168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334135/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-334135/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-334135/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-334135/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-334135/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-334135/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334135" :question/id #uuid "6298a7a6-04fe-518a-893c-c39ced91d488" :question/math-academy-id "q-334135" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211/selection/15b4657625ed2d6348d1910905ba11a74d7f096abfae7cdc990a6b6d3be0d6a6" :answer/id #uuid "14fdaa89-d8d1-5bda-b1f0-a430347d1207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211/selection/b2a9df7c2292d64ff8366dec60a651d620414cdac4a9a4d0c7fc1ffdeed8ea00" :answer/id #uuid "b68b9f01-0a56-5d28-9a84-a6760b703190" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)x+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211/selection/362e8a755cb6a70efe8ca390bfbf570af601211f19fe6ea648649dafd894fcb6" :answer/id #uuid "d2723464-ea55-5449-95e8-793f88e80363" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211/selection/df8aa849dfe071e48d88a0984e24defa49d08fdba0bee092528867a4082ea6f0" :answer/id #uuid "da9570c5-8324-58ae-bdc5-de1a496ce8fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2c)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211/selection/05f97b0a353a462df5d14fbf5884d3aa56a2735a549ffc900fab61e1fe3a0b09" :answer/id #uuid "f2b2b8ce-e74d-5125-951e-d88dd4cd3702" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bab4eee6-1c29-5802-a040-f636dc0d6d85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334211/selection/05f97b0a353a462df5d14fbf5884d3aa56a2735a549ffc900fab61e1fe3a0b09" :answer-field/choices ["q-334211/selection/15b4657625ed2d6348d1910905ba11a74d7f096abfae7cdc990a6b6d3be0d6a6" "q-334211/selection/b2a9df7c2292d64ff8366dec60a651d620414cdac4a9a4d0c7fc1ffdeed8ea00" "q-334211/selection/362e8a755cb6a70efe8ca390bfbf570af601211f19fe6ea648649dafd894fcb6" "q-334211/selection/df8aa849dfe071e48d88a0984e24defa49d08fdba0bee092528867a4082ea6f0" "q-334211/selection/05f97b0a353a462df5d14fbf5884d3aa56a2735a549ffc900fab61e1fe3a0b09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334211" :question/id #uuid "200b86ba-363f-5353-8284-8f17ccfff444" :question/math-academy-id "q-334211" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,{y}^{′}>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p))}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c>0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p),$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c).\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334211/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334211"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209/selection/028e1c4038eefccf493ca51bef7be697422b753ca38acbb3f7f93e388f4be278" :answer/id #uuid "90828dbd-97ff-58fc-ac9e-ab44f434c958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={c}^{3}x+{e}^{c}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209/selection/15b4657625ed2d6348d1910905ba11a74d7f096abfae7cdc990a6b6d3be0d6a6" :answer/id #uuid "063bc775-e5ac-5449-9665-f7d7235af128" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209/selection/015a5e878a0a1822f573ceba2fe52e2b6183244f1128b94866e4ddef31dd6ceb" :answer/id #uuid "918ba645-4d28-539c-8015-05ef51603b9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+{e}^{2c}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209/selection/21d3ad8e3a752e546c8b29b5b95fe66b84980fd384991b503e579cdf4354fee3" :answer/id #uuid "475c37e3-7dea-5ed4-aa53-07ba8a990b5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+{e}^{c}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209/selection/ae7080dd617dfd79f62d0b6ff547a0057a5b9bfe391654059b49709de12e3ba2" :answer/id #uuid "9dad72d6-c6d3-5499-87ad-b7014d774d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x+{e}^{c}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a857ee0-e13d-5cab-b59e-3f67306b436b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334209/selection/21d3ad8e3a752e546c8b29b5b95fe66b84980fd384991b503e579cdf4354fee3" :answer-field/choices ["q-334209/selection/028e1c4038eefccf493ca51bef7be697422b753ca38acbb3f7f93e388f4be278" "q-334209/selection/15b4657625ed2d6348d1910905ba11a74d7f096abfae7cdc990a6b6d3be0d6a6" "q-334209/selection/015a5e878a0a1822f573ceba2fe52e2b6183244f1128b94866e4ddef31dd6ceb" "q-334209/selection/21d3ad8e3a752e546c8b29b5b95fe66b84980fd384991b503e579cdf4354fee3" "q-334209/selection/ae7080dd617dfd79f62d0b6ff547a0057a5b9bfe391654059b49709de12e3ba2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334209" :question/id #uuid "28d9a796-6415-5070-af00-f7842262a2dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334209" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{e}^{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{e}^{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{e}^{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334209/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334209"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-325651"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{p}^{2},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{c}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265/selection/05840ab4005eeb7d70f6fc03fa928b4038f222d23eff23cb15e9eaacde782646" :answer/id #uuid "408d1fd2-c8b8-5666-ab93-1e62a5837a20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x+7c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265/selection/1592b983d70aef5c97941503b411e343137f81970e55b4433bcc5c355fd75144" :answer/id #uuid "f31f574e-4b81-5f25-942a-3f092b39c937" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+7{c}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265/selection/b5d4550c4baaca4d142620dabefb3da2a36628fb2536f5a8110654b34f9d2b16" :answer/id #uuid "211c21e1-0df4-52d5-bb9d-8282ba386d94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx-7c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265/selection/3213667dbf6450e1a09e17adf73aac02ba49714608f8927646622a53fbfbca2a" :answer/id #uuid "abae75ab-64c2-501e-a440-d4b163e2878f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x-7c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265/selection/18cc9cc9872aa844fab5a236de918dc04865ad1f82c1da77355d646754deff4d" :answer/id #uuid "d07d1777-4202-56e4-b247-805f90e02249" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "46bfeada-20cb-5d64-b402-78d8a130158e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334265/selection/b5d4550c4baaca4d142620dabefb3da2a36628fb2536f5a8110654b34f9d2b16" :answer-field/choices ["q-334265/selection/05840ab4005eeb7d70f6fc03fa928b4038f222d23eff23cb15e9eaacde782646" "q-334265/selection/1592b983d70aef5c97941503b411e343137f81970e55b4433bcc5c355fd75144" "q-334265/selection/b5d4550c4baaca4d142620dabefb3da2a36628fb2536f5a8110654b34f9d2b16" "q-334265/selection/3213667dbf6450e1a09e17adf73aac02ba49714608f8927646622a53fbfbca2a" "q-334265/selection/18cc9cc9872aa844fab5a236de918dc04865ad1f82c1da77355d646754deff4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334265" :question/id #uuid "618dbf12-e8c4-5dd6-9a3f-db361be015f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-334265" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-7{y}^{′}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-7{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-7p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp-7p)}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-7{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp-7{p}^{′},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx-7c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334265/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334265"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205/selection/b65cc269e91d0acf71537630e42027b7bb438d2a9ce6af914198500b2ea241da" :answer/id #uuid "309f7400-90e1-5837-9119-e5b571314189" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx+4{c}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205/selection/92fa049eafe040a2fff987c56f5cbad7e68c767d762d349bc5a2b0da5fa95df0" :answer/id #uuid "4a718216-2ede-55da-b7cf-5cdf145de7f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx-{c}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205/selection/aa909d59aa2b2966f562ec4611029cb85366cefda5519435b9f660908ee90295" :answer/id #uuid "d93a1f7a-4ffd-5147-b32f-2cdfc1594730" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=cx-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205/selection/601cda3b16280e5fba3156c799359303c534ea92a0f72b09bec29dafe1162fa6" :answer/id #uuid "173418ee-ef16-52fc-9ff5-13da63479757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={c}^{3}x-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205/selection/93d0cc97cf5d396a98ff7c17bfca79f07c050da89622ce2f94b2b4c4840b1adc" :answer/id #uuid "36907a14-2e66-5a1a-b2c4-b938cb71f893" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ece273b9-a288-5f6e-883e-7299c985caf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334205/selection/aa909d59aa2b2966f562ec4611029cb85366cefda5519435b9f660908ee90295" :answer-field/choices ["q-334205/selection/b65cc269e91d0acf71537630e42027b7bb438d2a9ce6af914198500b2ea241da" "q-334205/selection/92fa049eafe040a2fff987c56f5cbad7e68c767d762d349bc5a2b0da5fa95df0" "q-334205/selection/aa909d59aa2b2966f562ec4611029cb85366cefda5519435b9f660908ee90295" "q-334205/selection/601cda3b16280e5fba3156c799359303c534ea92a0f72b09bec29dafe1162fa6" "q-334205/selection/93d0cc97cf5d396a98ff7c17bfca79f07c050da89622ce2f94b2b4c4840b1adc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334205" :question/id #uuid "5610e9ad-4bbc-520f-a9ee-8f20106ec156" :question/math-academy-id "q-334205" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4({y}^{′})^{3},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-2p{p}^{′}+12{p}^{2}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-2p+12{p}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334205/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334205"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452/selection/94444f0f561d10ac1e5cb92446e2d3a0412586f2fc08b1637189417b30b4d50c" :answer/id #uuid "68866aea-92bb-5b43-a8a0-03e92d95f059" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452/selection/9312625a816b556310a18614ce7831182b2dd6c425f997f7f7ff3d59e0273121" :answer/id #uuid "6175c781-349c-5a15-8ba5-d768492675fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452/selection/8d588716b4cd1f8f32dd22b84963b00fbe0174c6e64ffa44aaf9fdf3c1dc4681" :answer/id #uuid "16e123d2-23a7-5390-9302-77d8719ca933" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff" :answer/id #uuid "87f19102-4df1-52f8-b58a-f73c5c67901c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452/selection/61cbdea9e42da3b18aa46e0fc00e3abcae934450f781e829e16776b518e6e881" :answer/id #uuid "d43b3e6d-b73a-5814-852b-fd6910b29bea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae52fd74-1018-55c2-ac7b-11a61a0463e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334452/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff" :answer-field/choices ["q-334452/selection/94444f0f561d10ac1e5cb92446e2d3a0412586f2fc08b1637189417b30b4d50c" "q-334452/selection/9312625a816b556310a18614ce7831182b2dd6c425f997f7f7ff3d59e0273121" "q-334452/selection/8d588716b4cd1f8f32dd22b84963b00fbe0174c6e64ffa44aaf9fdf3c1dc4681" "q-334452/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff" "q-334452/selection/61cbdea9e42da3b18aa46e0fc00e3abcae934450f781e829e16776b518e6e881"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334452" :question/id #uuid "dc429d1e-7f4f-50b6-bac7-a69f2082aa23" :question/math-academy-id "q-334452" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+p-3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+p-3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+1=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+p-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+p-3 \\\\  & =(-1)p+p-3 \\\\  & =-p+p-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334452/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334452"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-334398"] :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-321239"] :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′}{e}^{p} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+{e}^{p}=0.$ Then, \n\n$$\nx=-{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{e}^{p}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{p})p+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-{e}^{p} \\\\ y=(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)\n$$\n\nSince $x=-{e}^{p}$ and ${e}^{p}>0,$ we must have $x<0.$ Thus, $p=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)$ is valid only for $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(1-p){e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)){e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402/selection/75ce932281bee79dd97c88acfec65940dbec8ef1efcd1e2d5338fea0581ca8c6" :answer/id #uuid "253af831-88c6-54ad-93ad-b1b8f9ef7516" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3{(\\frac{x}{9})}^{4/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402/selection/9d2cd1fb139e987bc5191eed9a603309757752d6a5b962a7bab0ce537b83a2b6" :answer/id #uuid "65c90fec-cc2d-5e60-aadc-a19419be502b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402/selection/ae46f9e2102f176564cdc437f8c4c0109d5980d5f0cf75768c5be48c9205a643" :answer/id #uuid "15b39a68-1707-562b-91d6-8d124c73151f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402/selection/bccf61f04ac5c7452283a7a4d60e1d4569f461d020c7e8a277c3e3e8f0bbbb1d" :answer/id #uuid "3f247143-3e5f-5a8e-8f17-0b10145d452f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{4/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402/selection/c7e42a61f90dfb6aee22774f1855cc8edd0d261490ff5f91a4016da929b1f26f" :answer/id #uuid "07bb1e7b-80ba-545e-ae1c-88f950d21bc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "212c49ce-46a0-5c4a-b839-6857fb18c4cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334402/selection/ae46f9e2102f176564cdc437f8c4c0109d5980d5f0cf75768c5be48c9205a643" :answer-field/choices ["q-334402/selection/75ce932281bee79dd97c88acfec65940dbec8ef1efcd1e2d5338fea0581ca8c6" "q-334402/selection/9d2cd1fb139e987bc5191eed9a603309757752d6a5b962a7bab0ce537b83a2b6" "q-334402/selection/ae46f9e2102f176564cdc437f8c4c0109d5980d5f0cf75768c5be48c9205a643" "q-334402/selection/bccf61f04ac5c7452283a7a4d60e1d4569f461d020c7e8a277c3e3e8f0bbbb1d" "q-334402/selection/c7e42a61f90dfb6aee22774f1855cc8edd0d261490ff5f91a4016da929b1f26f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334402" :question/id #uuid "5214463c-09d7-577f-9b86-6b65821b4dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334402" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{4})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-4{p}^{3}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-4{p}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-4{p}^{3}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp-{p}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{3})p-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =4{p}^{4}-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4{p}^{3} \\\\ y=3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np={(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{({(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3})}^{4} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334402/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334402"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454/selection/a9e474de7328400f06d3e4e3453a67737be3bbc1663040f600c88d418a72991f" :answer/id #uuid "b914e059-65ab-5e4a-9f49-8d79679f173a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454/selection/4f808b090797d29db64c1fd2085c91d86225fcfb8b323b22de821891e6f8c5b4" :answer/id #uuid "bd692684-4c6a-5b05-b776-07e319d9014c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454/selection/0865aa0691b70039e00a9179c4f23ced278796136055dfea815cab1c4d2ad152" :answer/id #uuid "cd4656f3-e415-582f-835d-66def5357205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454/selection/1110e06da605dd88a5920205e4fcca5b05e3860ce2de05bf2d1a56d882d1a551" :answer/id #uuid "81fb5a64-b713-5d60-8e64-141ea33c4553" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3-{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454/selection/cf77711589a6156a13595f12c63ef9c9c7be7d1ab7d181b850f786ca82994d1c" :answer/id #uuid "0fa9d9c4-b178-5146-99ba-4790f93d3b14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "78950484-4e7a-53ff-8cf3-8d22f49a0ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334454/selection/cf77711589a6156a13595f12c63ef9c9c7be7d1ab7d181b850f786ca82994d1c" :answer-field/choices ["q-334454/selection/a9e474de7328400f06d3e4e3453a67737be3bbc1663040f600c88d418a72991f" "q-334454/selection/4f808b090797d29db64c1fd2085c91d86225fcfb8b323b22de821891e6f8c5b4" "q-334454/selection/0865aa0691b70039e00a9179c4f23ced278796136055dfea815cab1c4d2ad152" "q-334454/selection/1110e06da605dd88a5920205e4fcca5b05e3860ce2de05bf2d1a56d882d1a551" "q-334454/selection/cf77711589a6156a13595f12c63ef9c9c7be7d1ab7d181b850f786ca82994d1c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334454" :question/id #uuid "44861a06-20aa-5d1c-9024-73e89bd2b681" :question/math-academy-id "q-334454" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+2{p}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+2{p}^{2}+3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+4p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+4p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+4p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-4p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+2{p}^{2}+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(-4p)p+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-4{p}^{2}+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4p \\\\ y=-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$ \n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334454/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334454"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21082"] :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y -xy' -\\ln(y')=0$\n\n- $y^4\\cos(x) +xyy' = y^2$\n\n- $y= xy' + 2y'$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = xy' + \\ln(y'),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\ng(y') = \\ln(y').\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny^3\\cos(x) +xy' = y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y = xy' + g(y').$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with \n\n$$\ng(y') = 2y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21413"] :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{-p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-{e}^{-p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-{e}^{-p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $p' = 0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np = c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ into $y = x p + e^{-p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = cx + e^{-c}\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21083"] :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2+1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x + 2p = 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx = -2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y = xp + p^2+1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
